marie: Quick report from debugging the Go generator stream UX: when we get an auth refresh mid-stream the resumed transport sometimes replays the last chunk and our sampleapps that append output get dupes. I put a focused repro in sampleapp/generator/handshake-replay.go. Anyone seen this pattern?

dave: Yep. The transport does a handshake ACK on reconnect and the server currently resends up to the last ACK boundary. If the client treats ACK as persistence it ends up double-appending.

anya: The tricky part for Go ergonomics is that users expect context cancellation to equate to "stop everything" while the service supports resume. That mismatch leads to either orphan goroutines (if we try to resume under the hood) or surprising aborts (if we stop and drop resume tokens).

leo: Proposal: expose three explicit primitives on the Go GeneratorStream: Abort(), PauseForResume() (returns resumeSig string), and Resume(resumeSig string). PauseForResume does not cancel the user's ctx; it quiesces internal readers and returns an opaque resumeSig the server understands. Abort forces immediate shutdown and sets Err() to context.Canceled.

marie: Example usage in sampleapp idea: user loop persists events with an idempotency key and on AuthExpired the SDK surfaces a ResumeSig. App can call Resume(resumeSig) to reconnect and the SDK will dedupe using the last persisted sequence id. Pseudo: ```go
if ev.Type == AuthExpired {
  sig := stream.PauseForResume()
  // persist progress
  if shouldResume {
    err := stream.Resume(sig)
  } else {
    stream.Abort()
  }
}
```

dave: Implementation notes: make Resume use a fresh transport but pass resumeSig in the handshake header. The stream should track lastSequenceSeen and keep a small replay window; document that Resume may replay 1-2 events depending on timing. Also avoid using channel close as broadcast; prefer context with cancellation + atomic flags for state transitions.

anya: SDK parity note: Node's stream.resume() returns a promise and Python raises an AuthExpired with a resume_token. Aligning semantics across SDKs would reduce customer confusion. For Go, explicit PauseForResume semantics communicates that user retains control and avoids implicit reconnects.

leo: CI caught flakes in generator_handshake_replay_test on main. I created https://ci/internal/9998 and marked the test flaky until we land dedupe logic. ci-bot: :robot_face: generator_handshake_replay_test failed 3/10; logs: https://ci/internal/9998/logs.

marie: Action items: leo -> open PR adding PauseForResume/Resume/Abort stubs and unit tests showing deterministic dupes; anya -> update sampleapp/generator to demonstrate persisted lastSequence + resume safe pattern; dave -> design doc on handshake header format and replay window; I'll draft a short "Streaming gotchas" note in the Go README explaining why ctx cancel != Abort and when to use each API.

leo: I'll also add an example that uses a small in-memory dedupe map keyed by event.SequenceID so new users can copy-paste.

marie: thanks all. scheduling a 20m sync tomorrow to align Node/Python owners and finalize resume token shape. :thumbsup:
